          "method_draft": "Use a coordinate setup instead of the sine rule. Place B = (0,0) and A = (4,0), since c = AB = 4. Because angle B = pi/6, write C = (a cos 30°, a sin 30°) = (a sqrt(3)/2, a/2), where a = BC. Then b = AC. Compute vectors at C: CA = (4 - a sqrt(3)/2, -a/2) and CB = (-a sqrt(3)/2, -a/2). The dot-product formula for angle C gives cos C = (CA · CB)/(|CA||CB|) = (a - 2 sqrt(3))/b = sqrt(5)/3. Also from the distance formula, b^2 = AC^2 = a^2 - 4 sqrt(3)a + 16 = (a - 2 sqrt(3))^2 + 4. Substitute a - 2 sqrt(3) = sqrt(5)b/3 to get b^2 = 5b^2/9 + 4, hence b = 3.",
